    <origin>Owner decision 2026-09-19 on D-1: "follow recommendation; for C if this is needed for the best solution then do not defer." Option C of the plugin-free answer to task 107. The prerequisite ordering was surfaced in notes/owner-decision-brief-2026-09-18.md § "Nothing is blocked" — re-point the UI gate BEFORE removing Create, or Manage Access vanishes for every user while the BFF's app-only write keeps working.</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>The UI and the server ask DIFFERENT questions about the same action. The server asks "do you have Write on this record?" and denies when it cannot tell. The PCF asks "do you have Create on the sprk_externalrecordaccess TABLE, globally?" and allows when it cannot tell. Make the client ask the server's question, with the server's fail direction — then, and only then, take Create away from human roles.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer — PCF controls, the external-access authorization path, and Dataverse security roles. You are narrowing who can open Manage Access; a wrong ordering removes the button from everyone.</role>

  <goal>The Manage Access affordance is gated on the same rule the server enforces (Write on the target record, FR-07), it is DISABLED when that rule cannot be evaluated, its two comments no longer claim a defense-in-depth gate that does not exist, and the now-unnecessary Create privilege is recorded for removal in an operator step that runs AFTER the code ships.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>D-1 ruled out Dataverse plugins repo-wide. Option C of the replacement answer is to stop relying on a table-level Create privilege as the proxy for "may grant", because the real rule lives on the server and is per-record.

🔴 THE BINDING ORDERING, AND WHY. `SDAP User (Core)` currently holds Create = Yes on `sprk_externalrecordaccess` (entity-schema.md:184-190). If that privilege is removed while the client still gates on it, `computeCanGrantAccess` returns false for every user, the person icon disables, and Manage Access disappears — while the BFF's app-only write path keeps working, so nothing fails loudly. Code first, config second. This is not a preference.

VERIFIED FACTS (each checked 2026-09-19 against source; verify anyway — this project has 19 prior instances of premise rot per `notes/task-accuracy-audit-2026-09-18.md:9`, and the code has won every time):
- THE CLIENT'S QUESTION. `src/client/pcf/TrackingFieldTrio/index.ts:462-473`: `computeCanGrantAccess()` calls `utils.hasEntityPrivilege(EXTERNAL_ACCESS_ENTITY, /* Create */ 1, /* Global */ 3)` inside a try, and on throw or missing API falls through to `return true` at `:472`. Called at `:255`; the entity constant is at `:159`; a back-reference comment sits at `:869`.
- THE SERVER'S QUESTION. `DelegationRuleFilter` (`src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/ExternalAccess/DelegationRuleFilter.cs:81`, an IEndpointFilter) is attached GROUP-level by `AddDelegationRuleFilter()` at `Api/ExternalAccess/ExternalAccessEndpoints.cs:126` — deliberately group-level, not per-route (`:116`), with a request-type to target-record map in its remarks (`:119`). The contract, per spec.md FR-07 (`:57`): "a caller without Write on the target record receives 403". `SetRecordShareExpiryEndpoint.cs:33` states it as "Write on THIS record".
- THE ANSWER MACHINE, AND ITS FAIL DIRECTION. `Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/CallerRecordAccessProbe.cs` asks Dataverse `GET systemusers({principal})/Microsoft.Dynamics.CRM.RetrievePrincipalAccess(Target=@p1)` (`:414`) over OBO — NOT app-only (`DelegationRuleFilter.cs:77`) — and FAILS CLOSED on every degraded path: rejected exchange, WhoAmI unavailable, probe unavailable, probe throws (`:45`, `:52`, `:478` "Denying."). It is registered with `AddHttpClient&lt;CallerRecordAccessProbe&gt;` at `Infrastructure/DI/ExternalAccessModule.cs:120`.
- NO CLIENT CAN REACH IT. Its consumers are four filters (`EntityAccessFilter`, `RecordRouteAccessAuthorizationFilter`, `DelegationRuleFilter`, and `ContainerDocumentAuthorizationFilter` which deliberately does not call it, `:91`) and three endpoints (`UnsecureProjectEndpoint`, `ProvisionProjectEndpoint`, `InternalShareEndpoints`). NO endpoint returns the caller's rights to a client. That is why this task adds surface — see the justification.
- 🔴 THE FAIL-OPEN'S STATED REASON IS FALSE. `index.ts:459-460` and `AccessGrantModal/types.ts:144` both justify the fail-open by citing "a SECOND, defense-in-depth gate" in the modal. But `AccessGrantModal.tsx:232` defaults `canGrantAccess = true` and `:313`/`:630` read THE SAME PROP, and `TrackingFieldTrio.tsx:244` is `const grantEnabled = canGrantAccess !== false`, so `undefined` enables. Two gates reading one value are one gate. The CONCLUSION still holds — the BFF enforces regardless — but the stated reason does not, and a comment vouching for a guarantee its neighbour does not provide is how this defect survived review.
- NO TEST PINS IT. `projects/code-quality-and-assurance-r3/workstreams/pcf-controls/design.md:242` records that five live controls have no tests at all, naming this gate (`index.ts:462`, `return true` at `:472`).

⚠️ CROSS-PROJECT COLLISION — HANDLE IT, DO NOT DISCOVER IT. That same workstream's `design.md:180` says: "fail-open is the documented availability fallback with server-side BFF enforcement behind it. D7-01 TESTS the behavior; switching to fail-closed is an owner-input behavior change outside this workstream." THIS TASK IS THAT OWNER-INPUT CHANGE. That project is initialized but NOT executing (`projects/INDEX.md:53`, last commit 2026-08-06), so no such test exists yet. If it later writes D7-01 asserting fail-OPEN, it will pin the retired rule — exactly task 114's defect (`InternalUserShareContractTests.cs:94` asserts a 422 the owner retired). Note also that `src/client/pcf/**` is NOT on the `/conflict-check` hot-path watchlist (its columns are BFF / SpaarkeAi / CI / Skill-Directives), so no tooling will surface this for you.

THE CREATE REMOVAL IS CONFIGURATION, NOT CODE. `SDAP User (Core)` appears in exactly ONE file in the entire repo — `entity-schema.md` — so there is no solution XML or role definition to edit. Removing Create is a Dataverse security-role change performed by an operator. This task delivers the code, the doc correction, and the runbook step; it does not and cannot perform the role edit.</background>
    <relevant-files>
