    <patterns>
      <pattern name="The rule to mirror" location="src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/ExternalAccess/DelegationRuleFilter.cs">Read its remarks for the request-type to target-record map — the client must ask about the SAME record the server would authorize. Task 008's note records a ready-made perturbation: detaching `.AddDelegationRuleFilter()` from the group makes 17 of 36 tests fail.</pattern>
      <pattern name="Fail-closed probing" location="src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/CallerRecordAccessProbe.cs">Every degraded path denies (`:45`, `:52`, `:478`). Copy that posture into the client gate; note the probe deliberately conflates "record absent" with "no access" (`EntityAccessFilter.cs:361`) — do not try to un-conflate it here.</pattern>
    </patterns>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="prescriptive">
    <step order="0">Verify the premises above at HEAD, especially `computeCanGrantAccess` at `index.ts:462-473` and the group-level filter attachment at `ExternalAccessEndpoints.cs:126`. Confirm no client-callable rights endpoint has appeared since 2026-09-19; if one has, extend it instead of adding one.</step>
    <step order="1">Add the caller-rights endpoint beside the external-access routes, reusing `CallerRecordAccessProbe` over OBO, returning the caller's rights on one (entity, recordId). State the Placement Justification.</step>
    <step order="2">Re-point `computeCanGrantAccess` onto that endpoint — Write on THIS record — and make it FAIL CLOSED. Because the answer is now asynchronous while `updateView` is synchronous, resolve it into the control's state and re-render, mirroring `EmailProcessingMonitor`'s documented auth-init pattern rather than blocking.</step>
    <step order="3">Delete the false "second, defense-in-depth gate" justification at `index.ts:459-460` and `AccessGrantModal/types.ts:144`, replacing it with what is actually true: the modal's check reads the same prop, and the real backstop is `DelegationRuleFilter`.</step>
    <step order="4">Update `TrackingFieldTrio.toolbar.test.tsx:124` and add the first tests for the gate itself: Write present, Write absent, and probe-unavailable (must DISABLE). Run the UI tests below.</step>
    <step order="5">Correct `entity-schema.md`: the role table (`:184-190`) records that Create is no longer required by the UI, business rule 2 (`:216`) no longer prescribes a pre-create plugin (D-1 banned plugins repo-wide), and business rule 4 (`:220`) no longer says expiry is "enforced NOWHERE" (tasks 097/107 retired that).</step>
    <step order="6">Write the operator runbook step for removing Create from `SDAP User (Core)` and the other human roles, explicitly gated on steps 1-4 being DEPLOYED. Do not perform the role change. Then verify: build, full suite, ArchTests, publish size vs a fresh master, PCF version bump, notes, TASK-INDEX row.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If anything OTHER than this UI gate reads Create on `sprk_externalrecordaccess` — another control, a form script, a flow, a view — removing it is no longer safe in one step. STOP and report every reader before the runbook step is written.</trigger>
    <trigger>If the PCF context cannot supply an OBO-authenticated call at gate-evaluation time (e.g. the gate must be computed before `authInit` resolves), STOP rather than reintroducing a fail-open to paper over the timing. The acceptable answers are "render disabled until the probe answers" or an owner decision — not a silent default to enabled.</trigger>
    <trigger>If `code-quality-and-assurance-r3` has begun executing its pcf-controls workstream and D7-01 has landed a test asserting fail-open, coordinate before changing the behaviour: two projects asserting opposite rules on one gate is a merge conflict that a green suite will hide on each side separately.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>`CallerRecordAccessProbe` already answers exactly this question server-side over OBO, and `DelegationRuleFilter` already enforces it. Its consumers are four endpoint filters and three endpoints — enumerated by grep on 2026-09-19 — and none of them returns the answer to a client. On the client side, `hasEntityPrivilege` is the only existing mechanism, and it can only see TABLE-level privileges, never per-record rights.</existing>
    <extension>The probe itself is reused, not rebuilt — the new surface is one thin route that exposes an answer the server already computes. It cannot be avoided by extending an existing endpoint: every current external-access route is a WRITE gated by the group filter, so asking one of them "may I?" would mean performing the action. A host-context alternative (calling `RetrievePrincipalAccess` from `context.webAPI`) was considered and rejected — it would put an authorization derivation in the client, which is the pattern this project exists to remove, and the BFF already holds the OBO credential.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Two concrete failures. (1) The gate answers the wrong question: a user with Create on the table but NO Write on a confidential matter is offered Manage Access, clicks through, and gets a 403 from the server — the affordance promises an action the caller cannot perform, on exactly the records where that matters most. (2) It fails OPEN: when `hasEntityPrivilege` is unavailable it returns `true` (`:472`), so the affordance appears for everyone, and the comment explaining why that is safe cites a second gate that reads the same value and therefore does not exist. Without this task, D-1 option C cannot be executed at all — removing Create would hide Manage Access for every user.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

  <ui-tests>
    <test name="Manage Access is offered when the caller has Write on the record">
      <url>https://{org}.crm.dynamics.com/main.aspx?appid={app-id}&amp;pagetype=entityrecord&amp;etn=sprk_project</url>
      <steps>
        <step>Open a project record the signed-in user has Write on</step>
        <step>Verify the person (Manage Access) icon in the TrackingFieldTrio toolbar is ENABLED</step>
        <step>Click it and verify AccessGrantModal opens</step>
        <step>Check the console for errors</step>
      </steps>
      <expected>Icon enabled, modal opens, no console errors</expected>
    </test>
    <test name="Manage Access is withheld when the caller lacks Write on the record">
      <steps>
        <step>Open a project record the signed-in user can Read but not Write</step>
        <step>Verify the person icon is DISABLED and not a dead click</step>
        <step>Verify the email icon is unaffected (it is gated independently)</step>
      </steps>
      <expected>Grant icon disabled; email icon still enabled; no dead click</expected>
    </test>
    <test name="Probe unavailable DISABLES the affordance (the fail-direction inversion)">
      <steps>
        <step>Block or fail the caller-rights endpoint for the session</step>
        <step>Reload the form and verify the person icon is DISABLED</step>
        <step>Verify a diagnostic is logged rather than failing silently</step>
      </steps>
      <expected>Icon disabled — the pre-change behaviour was ENABLED, and this assertion is the regression guard for that inversion</expected>
    </test>
    <test name="Dark Mode Compliance (ADR-021)">
      <steps>
        <step>Toggle dark mode in D365 settings</step>
        <step>Verify the disabled and enabled icon states both adapt (no hard-coded white/black)</step>
        <step>Verify icons use currentColor</step>
      </steps>
      <expected>All colors resolve from Fluent UI v9 semantic tokens per ADR-021</expected>
    </test>
  </ui-tests>

