  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">The new endpoint returns the CALLER's rights on one (entity, recordId) over OBO, reusing `CallerRecordAccessProbe`; it adds no second `RetrievePrincipalAccess` caller anywhere in the codebase (verified by grep).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A caller WITH Write on the target record sees the Manage Access affordance enabled.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A caller WITHOUT Write on the target record sees it disabled, and clicking is not a dead click.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">🔴 When the rights answer cannot be obtained — endpoint unreachable, throws, auth not initialised, or the record is absent — the affordance is DISABLED. This is the inversion: the pre-change code returned `true` at `index.ts:472`.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`hasEntityPrivilege` is no longer consulted for the grant gate anywhere in `TrackingFieldTrio` (verified by grep), and the `Create`/`Global` privilege constants for that purpose are gone.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`TrackingFieldTrio.toolbar.test.tsx:124` ("defaults the person icon to enabled when canGrantAccess is omitted") is updated so no test asserts the retired fail-open default, and a test exists for the new fail-closed default.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The comments at `index.ts:459-460` and `AccessGrantModal/types.ts:144` no longer describe the modal's check as a SECOND, independent, defense-in-depth gate; they state that it reads the same prop and that `DelegationRuleFilter` is the real backstop.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`AccessGrantModal` still receives a boolean and remains entity-agnostic — no entity name, record id or probe call is introduced into the shared component (ADR-012).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Server enforcement is unchanged: `DelegationRuleFilter` still gates every external-access write, and the new endpoint is never the mechanism that permits a write. Detaching `.AddDelegationRuleFilter()` still reds the suite (task 008 recorded 17 of 36).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`entity-schema.md` is corrected on three points: the role table no longer implies the UI requires Create, business rule 2 no longer prescribes a pre-create plugin, and business rule 4 no longer claims expiry is enforced nowhere.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The operator runbook step for removing Create from `SDAP User (Core)` (and the other human roles) exists in the task notes, explicitly gated on the code being DEPLOYED, and the task itself performs NO role change.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Every reader of Create on `sprk_externalrecordaccess` has been enumerated and reported; if any reader other than this gate exists, the escalation fired rather than the runbook being written.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The PCF version is bumped in all four locations and shown in the footer; the control is built with `npm run build:prod`.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The four `ui-tests` above pass, including the dark-mode check.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Build green; full suite green; ArchTests green; publish size reported against a fresh master with equal file counts on both sides and ≤60 MB; no new HIGH CVE.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Test scope: cover the stated contract plus the edge cases named above. Anything beyond that needs a one-line justification in the task notes. Do not add near-identical happy-path variants.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Authored 2026-09-19 (session 17) via /task-create. Option C of owner decision D-1; siblings are task 107 (option A, invert the read default) and task 117 (option B, the reconciliation job). None blocks another, but this one has an internal ordering that is binding.

Why this is more than a privilege swap: the UI and the server have been asking different questions about the same action, with OPPOSITE fail directions — the server denies what it cannot evaluate, the client permits it. That asymmetry is the defect; removing `Create` is merely what becomes possible once it is fixed.

⚠️ The fail-open's stated justification is false and was believed. Two files call the modal's check "a SECOND, defense-in-depth gate" while it reads the same prop. The conclusion it supports happens to be true for another reason (the BFF enforces regardless), which is exactly why nobody checked — a comment that vouches for a guarantee its neighbour does not provide teaches the next reader to discount its true neighbours too.

Coordination: this supersedes `code-quality-and-assurance-r3`'s pcf-controls finding D7-01 as written (`workstreams/pcf-controls/design.md:180`), which names the fail-closed switch as owner input outside its scope and would otherwise TEST the fail-open. That project is initialized but not executing (`projects/INDEX.md:53`, last commit 2026-08-06). Note `src/client/pcf/**` is NOT on the `/conflict-check` hot-path watchlist, so this coordination is carried here rather than by tooling.</notes>

